WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.351
Welcome to Module 2 of the
Microsoft Automation learning path:

2
00:00:04.351 --> 00:00:07.412
Microsoft Entra ID
through Microsoft Graph.

3
00:00:07.412 --> 00:00:12.563
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 02 builds the Entra ID target,

4
00:00:12.563 --> 00:00:18.487
and Book 04 puts access behind Conditional
Access, PIM and application governance.

5
00:00:18.487 --> 00:00:21.001
Both ask for evidence
a script can collect.

6
00:00:21.001 --> 00:00:25.550
In this module you'll map each of those
evidence questions to the Graph API

7
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.100
that answers it, its least-privileged
permission, and the shape of its data.

8
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.507
Then you'll run five read-only checks,

9
00:00:32.507 --> 00:00:36.038
in Python and PowerShell,
against a fictional tenant.

10
00:00:36.038 --> 00:00:41.476
They're illustrative: unit-tested against
fixtures, not proven against live tenants.

11
00:00:41.476 --> 00:00:44.459
Module 1's foundations, the right cloud,

12
00:00:44.459 --> 00:00:48.920
sign-in without a secret,
and paging, apply throughout.

13
00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:53.645
Why script it?

14
00:00:53.645 --> 00:00:57.033
Your ISSO asks the same
questions every month.

15
00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:58.282
Who hasn't signed in?

16
00:00:58.282 --> 00:01:00.769
What do the Conditional
Access policies say?

17
00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:02.933
Who holds a privileged role permanently?

18
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:05.157
Which applications still have passwords?

19
00:01:05.157 --> 00:01:09.019
And who can't yet use phishing-resistant
multifactor authentication?

20
00:01:09.019 --> 00:01:12.867
Too often, someone answers by
clicking through the admin center and

21
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:16.645
pasting screenshots, and the answer
can't be reproduced next month.

22
00:01:16.645 --> 00:01:20.979
In this module each question becomes
a Graph call with a least-privileged

23
00:01:20.979 --> 00:01:25.598
permission, and five of them become
read-only checks you can rerun on a schedule,

24
00:01:25.598 --> 00:01:29.380
with output an assessor can diff
from one month to the next.

25
00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.963
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

26
00:01:35.963 --> 00:01:38.667
which takes about ninety-five
minutes with the lab.

27
00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:41.356
You'll name the Graph
API and least-privileged

28
00:01:41.356 --> 00:01:44.468
permission for each of the
books' evidence questions.

29
00:01:44.468 --> 00:01:49.582
You'll read sign-in activity, group
membership, Conditional Access, PIM schedules,

30
00:01:49.582 --> 00:01:54.467
application credentials and registration
details, and know the trap in each.

31
00:01:54.467 --> 00:01:58.342
You'll explain why a script checks
Entra's logs but doesn't keep them.

32
00:01:58.342 --> 00:02:01.980
You'll say what differs
in GCC High and DoD.

33
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:06.855
And you'll run the five checks and read
each finding against its ScubaGear policy.

34
00:02:06.855 --> 00:02:08.605
You need Module 1 first.

35
00:02:08.605 --> 00:02:15.480
Lab 1 needs no tenant; Lab 2 needs a
test tenant with Entra ID P1 or P2.

36
00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.364
Here are six of the module's
sixteen evidence questions,

37
00:02:22.364 --> 00:02:26.792
with the Graph call and the least-privileged
application permission for each.

38
00:02:26.792 --> 00:02:31.032
Who signed in, and when: list users
with sign-in activity selected,

39
00:02:31.032 --> 00:02:35.192
which needs User dot Read dot All
plus AuditLog dot Read dot All.

40
00:02:35.192 --> 00:02:37.748
Who's in a group, nested groups included:

41
00:02:37.748 --> 00:02:41.742
transitive members, with
GroupMember dot ReadBasic dot All.

42
00:02:41.742 --> 00:02:45.767
What the Conditional Access policies
say: Policy dot Read dot All.

43
00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:48.967
Who holds a role now: the
role assignment schedules.

44
00:02:48.967 --> 00:02:54.005
Which applications have secrets or
certificates: Application dot Read dot All.

45
00:02:54.005 --> 00:02:58.830
And who registered which authentication
methods: the registration details report.

46
00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:04.042
Every one of these is generally
available in Graph version one point oh,

47
00:03:04.042 --> 00:03:07.223
and each page lists it
in the global service,

48
00:03:07.223 --> 00:03:11.030
which GCC uses, and in the
GCC High and DoD clouds.

49
00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:19.605
Sign-in activity is how a script finds
dormant accounts, and it has traps.

50
00:03:19.605 --> 00:03:23.568
Use both dates, the interactive
and the non-interactive sign-in,

51
00:03:23.568 --> 00:03:28.293
because Microsoft notes some users
reach resources only through clients.

52
00:03:28.293 --> 00:03:33.592
It needs Entra ID P1 or P2 and the
AuditLog dot Read dot All permission.

53
00:03:33.592 --> 00:03:36.705
Selecting it caps the page
at five hundred users.

54
00:03:36.705 --> 00:03:39.455
And it's returned only when
you name it in select.

55
00:03:39.455 --> 00:03:43.199
For groups, transitive members
is effective membership,

56
00:03:43.199 --> 00:03:46.305
nested groups included;
members is direct only.

57
00:03:46.305 --> 00:03:50.330
The transitive call costs five
resource units against three.

58
00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.330
The basic membership read
permission is enough,

59
00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:57.530
and members of a type you can't read
come back with only an ID and a type.

60
00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:02.330
For change tracking, the users delta
call follows Module 1's pattern.

61
00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.722
Conditional Access is the
easiest read in the module:

62
00:04:09.722 --> 00:04:12.205
it needs only Policy dot Read dot All.

63
00:04:12.205 --> 00:04:14.996
Each policy's state is enabled, disabled,

64
00:04:14.996 --> 00:04:19.393
or enabled for reporting but not
enforced, which is report-only.

65
00:04:19.393 --> 00:04:23.560
Book 04's rollout depends on knowing
which policies are report-only;

66
00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:27.281
its phishing-resistant policy
for everyone starts that way.

67
00:04:27.281 --> 00:04:28.630
The collector reads.

68
00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:32.618
The ca-export check writes one
sorted JSON file per policy,

69
00:04:32.618 --> 00:04:36.605
so any change shows up as a
readable diff between two exports.

70
00:04:36.605 --> 00:04:39.247
Writing is different:
creating a policy needs

71
00:04:39.247 --> 00:04:42.167
the Conditional Access
read-write permission too.

72
00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:46.575
Book 04 keeps the policies as code,
so as design guidance, a pipeline

73
00:04:46.575 --> 00:04:51.910
holds the write permission and the
evidence collector holds only the read.

74
00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:59.117
Privileged Identity Management's
current APIs are under

75
00:04:59.117 --> 00:05:02.647
role management slash directory,
and two schedules matter.

76
00:05:02.647 --> 00:05:05.884
Eligibility schedules say
who may activate a role.

77
00:05:05.884 --> 00:05:08.510
Assignment schedules say who holds it now.

78
00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:13.117
The assignment type is Assigned for a
direct assignment, or Activated for an

79
00:05:13.117 --> 00:05:16.480
activation, and the member
type says whether it's direct,

80
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.235
through a group, or inherited.

81
00:05:18.235 --> 00:05:23.123
An assignment's end is in its expiration,
whose type can be no expiration.

82
00:05:23.123 --> 00:05:28.002
Put those together: an Assigned schedule
with no expiration is permanent active,

83
00:05:28.002 --> 00:05:32.810
which ScubaGear's policy seven point four
forbids for highly privileged roles.

84
00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:37.585
Role template IDs are stable; Global
Administrator's is on the slide.

85
00:05:37.585 --> 00:05:42.210
Changes need a read-write permission,
which the collector never asks for.

86
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.782
An application's secrets and certificates
each carry a start and an end date,

87
00:05:50.782 --> 00:05:55.680
in U T C, and those dates are enough to
test ScubaGear's three application rules:

88
00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:00.502
no application passwords, passwords
valid for at most a hundred eighty days,

89
00:06:00.502 --> 00:06:04.635
and certificates valid for at most
three hundred sixty-five days.

90
00:06:04.635 --> 00:06:08.934
Microsoft's app management policies
enforce the same limits going forward;

91
00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:12.073
the check finds what existed
before they were turned on.

92
00:06:12.073 --> 00:06:16.705
Two related reads support consent review:
who has been given an application's roles,

93
00:06:16.705 --> 00:06:19.725
and which delegated permissions
have been consented,

94
00:06:19.725 --> 00:06:22.230
which needs Directory dot Read dot All.

95
00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.093
Logs first.

96
00:06:27.093 --> 00:06:31.117
Directory audits and sign-ins both
need AuditLog dot Read dot All,

97
00:06:31.117 --> 00:06:34.917
and the Conditional Access
policies applied to a sign-in show

98
00:06:34.917 --> 00:06:38.643
up only for callers allowed to
read Conditional Access data.

99
00:06:38.643 --> 00:06:42.090
Entra keeps audit and
sign-in logs for seven days

100
00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:45.193
on the free tier and
thirty days with P1 or P2.

101
00:06:45.193 --> 00:06:49.804
That's why ScubaGear requires sending the
logs to the security operations center.

102
00:06:49.804 --> 00:06:52.843
A Graph script checks
logs; it doesn't keep them.

103
00:06:52.843 --> 00:06:58.529
Authentication methods: the registration
details report gives one row per user,

104
00:06:58.529 --> 00:07:03.706
with MFA registration, admin status,
user type and the methods registered.

105
00:07:03.706 --> 00:07:07.736
One caution: Microsoft documents
the method names only by example,

106
00:07:07.736 --> 00:07:12.133
so a count of phishing-resistant users
has to choose which names to count,

107
00:07:12.133 --> 00:07:15.290
and should be confirmed
against a real report.

108
00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.390
Five of those reads are built as checks.

109
00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:26.449
Stale-users lists enabled members with no
sign-in of either kind in ninety days,

110
00:07:26.449 --> 00:07:29.328
and never-signed-in
accounts older than that.

111
00:07:29.328 --> 00:07:33.748
Ca-export writes every policy as
sorted JSON with counts by state,

112
00:07:33.748 --> 00:07:37.378
evidence for ScubaGear's
Conditional Access settings.

113
00:07:37.378 --> 00:07:42.846
Pim-report counts Global Administrators
and lists every permanent active assignment

114
00:07:42.846 --> 00:07:47.865
to a role you name as privileged, for policies
seven point one and seven point four.

115
00:07:47.865 --> 00:07:50.390
App-credentials lists every secret and

116
00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:53.527
certificate with its
lifetime and days to expiry.

117
00:07:53.527 --> 00:07:57.492
And mfa-registration counts
members without MFA or without a

118
00:07:57.492 --> 00:08:01.766
phishing-resistant method, and
names administrators without one.

119
00:08:01.766 --> 00:08:04.627
The permissions for all
five are a reader's set.

120
00:08:04.627 --> 00:08:06.502
None of them can change the tenant,

121
00:08:06.502 --> 00:08:09.550
and you grant only the ones
for the checks you run.

122
00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:14.025
Try it.

123
00:08:14.025 --> 00:08:19.119
In the lab, you run each check against a
fictional tenant with the from-json option,

124
00:08:19.119 --> 00:08:23.225
which evaluates a saved Graph
response instead of calling Graph.

125
00:08:23.225 --> 00:08:25.166
Here's step three: the PIM report,

126
00:08:25.166 --> 00:08:28.375
with the Privileged Role
Administrator role added.

127
00:08:28.375 --> 00:08:31.112
Global Administrator is always included.

128
00:08:31.112 --> 00:08:34.103
The fixture has three
Global Administrators,

129
00:08:34.103 --> 00:08:38.038
which is within two to eight, so
policy seven point one passes.

130
00:08:38.038 --> 00:08:41.798
But two principals hold
privileged roles permanently,

131
00:08:41.798 --> 00:08:45.313
with no expiration, so policy
seven point four fails.

132
00:08:45.313 --> 00:08:50.511
The PowerShell function returns exactly
the same JSON, and the Pester tests compare

133
00:08:50.511 --> 00:08:54.987
every PowerShell check with the Python
results, so the two can't drift apart.

134
00:08:54.987 --> 00:08:57.930
The lab runs all five checks this way.

135
00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:07.042
Lab 2 runs the same checks read-only
against your own test tenant,

136
00:09:07.042 --> 00:09:13.618
worldwide or GCC, never production, with
Entra ID P1 or P2 for sign-in and PIM data.

137
00:09:13.618 --> 00:09:17.170
Register one app in the tenant's
own cloud with a certificate,

138
00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:20.580
and grant only the read
permissions for the checks you run;

139
00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:24.630
the module lists them per check,
straight from graph-calls dot JSON.

140
00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:29.680
Run each task in Python, or connect in
PowerShell and run the same task there.

141
00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.505
The client checks the token
first: a missing permission stops

142
00:09:33.505 --> 00:09:37.330
the run before any Graph call,
and an extra one prints a warning.

143
00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:39.843
Compare each result with the admin center.

144
00:09:39.843 --> 00:09:44.561
Then back it out: remove the permissions,
delete the app registration and its

145
00:09:44.561 --> 00:09:49.354
certificate, and delete the saved output,
which holds user names and policies.

146
00:09:49.354 --> 00:09:51.870
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

147
00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:56.932
Where are agencies?

148
00:09:56.932 --> 00:10:01.371
Most on this path run a GCC tenant
synchronized from Active Directory,

149
00:10:01.371 --> 00:10:06.038
with some Conditional Access policies
still report-only, permanent role

150
00:10:06.038 --> 00:10:10.858
assignments left from before PIM, and
applications that still hold secrets.

151
00:10:10.858 --> 00:10:14.335
The APIs are the same in GCC High and DoD;

152
00:10:14.335 --> 00:10:18.357
the endpoints differ,
and so do some features.

153
00:10:18.357 --> 00:10:23.030
Microsoft's U S Government feature
table lists Conditional Access, PIM,

154
00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:28.256
access reviews, entitlement management
and Lifecycle Workflows as available,

155
00:10:28.256 --> 00:10:32.770
HR-driven provisioning as partial,
and two features as not available.

156
00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:37.608
GCC uses the commercial Entra service,
so the table doesn't apply to it.

157
00:10:37.608 --> 00:10:41.064
One Azure Government difference
matters for the export:

158
00:10:41.064 --> 00:10:45.034
multifactor authentication's
Trusted IPs isn't supported there,

159
00:10:45.034 --> 00:10:49.446
so network-based exceptions live in
Conditional Access named locations.

160
00:10:49.446 --> 00:10:51.770
The interim paths are on the right.

161
00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:56.907
The federal requirement.

162
00:10:56.907 --> 00:11:01.711
BOD 25-01 makes ScubaGear's
mandatory Entra ID policies

163
00:11:01.711 --> 00:11:06.346
required for in-scope tenants,
with continuous reporting to CISA.

164
00:11:06.346 --> 00:11:10.704
Four of the checks produce evidence for
those: the Conditional Access settings,

165
00:11:10.704 --> 00:11:14.469
the Global Administrator count,
permanent privileged assignments,

166
00:11:14.469 --> 00:11:16.583
and phishing-resistant registration.

167
00:11:16.583 --> 00:11:20.420
In NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three terms,

168
00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:25.085
that's account management, A C two;
least privilege for privileged accounts;

169
00:11:25.085 --> 00:11:28.696
multifactor authentication;
and continuous monitoring.

170
00:11:28.696 --> 00:11:32.061
M-22-09's phishing-resistant
requirement is what

171
00:11:32.061 --> 00:11:34.833
the registration check
measures progress toward.

172
00:11:34.833 --> 00:11:39.482
Book 02's Appendix A has which policies
are mandatory, and the mapping.

173
00:11:39.482 --> 00:11:44.505
The evidence an ISSO keeps: each check's
dated output with the app's permissions,

174
00:11:44.505 --> 00:11:46.900
the Conditional Access export in Git,

175
00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:50.970
and the findings with the change
records that closed them.

176
00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:56.695
Check yourself with two questions.

177
00:11:56.695 --> 00:12:01.520
First: an Assigned role schedule has
an expiration type of no expiration.

178
00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:02.320
What is it?

179
00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:04.635
The answer is b: it's permanent active,

180
00:12:04.635 --> 00:12:08.107
which ScubaGear forbids for
highly privileged roles.

181
00:12:08.107 --> 00:12:10.481
Second: why doesn't a Graph script replace

182
00:12:10.481 --> 00:12:13.483
sending the logs to the
security operations center?

183
00:12:13.483 --> 00:12:14.482
The answer is a.

184
00:12:14.482 --> 00:12:19.149
Entra keeps audit and sign-in logs
for seven days on the free tier and

185
00:12:19.149 --> 00:12:23.732
thirty days with P1 or P2, so the
record has to be kept somewhere else.

186
00:12:23.732 --> 00:12:29.000
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

187
00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:33.575
To sum up.

188
00:12:33.575 --> 00:12:36.883
You mapped the books'
Entra ID evidence questions

189
00:12:36.883 --> 00:12:40.513
to Graph APIs and their
least-privileged permissions.

190
00:12:40.513 --> 00:12:44.164
You learned the traps in sign-in
activity, group membership,

191
00:12:44.164 --> 00:12:48.337
PIM schedules, application
credentials and registration details.

192
00:12:48.337 --> 00:12:52.562
You saw why a script checks
Entra's logs but doesn't keep them,

193
00:12:52.562 --> 00:12:55.062
and what differs in GCC High and DoD.

194
00:12:55.062 --> 00:12:58.125
And you ran the five checks
in Python and PowerShell.

195
00:12:58.125 --> 00:13:02.813
Book 04's Chapter 10 assembles
the evidence package for a period.

196
00:13:02.813 --> 00:13:07.615
Next is Module 3, Intune through
Microsoft Graph: devices, compliance,

197
00:13:07.615 --> 00:13:12.670
configuration and Autopilot, which
APIs are only in beta, and five checks

198
00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:17.800
mapped to NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three Moderate.
